UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036943919?origin=resultslist
AD  - Jakarta State Polytechnic, Informatics and Computer Engineering, Depok, Indonesia
AB  - Facing the financial management challenges encountered by millennials and Gen Z, coupled with the lack of planning and budgeting features in existing financial applications, this research has successfully designed and built a functional and relevant Android-based personal finance management application. This application innovatively implements the Japanese Kakeibo method to not only record transactions but also to facilitate users in creating category-based budgets, setting savings targets, and, most importantly, conducting structured monthly financial reflections. Developed using the Waterfall methodology, the application implements clean architecture with MVVM using Kotlin and Jetpack Compose on the client side, and Laravel for the REST API. It has undergone comprehensive testing. The results show 100% functionality as expected, a good usability level with an average System Usability Scale (SUS) score of 70.07, and strong content validity, evidenced by a Scale-Content Validity Index Average (S-CVI/Ave) score of 0.91 and a Universal Agreement (S-CVI/UA) score of 0.80 from expert evaluation. Thus, this research successfully developed a tested, user-accepted, and relevant technological solution that helps users achieve their financial goals in a more structured, planned manner. © 2025 IEEE.
